WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.000
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.530
First, let's talk about the raster and vector
graphics.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.530
What do these terms mean exactly?

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.170
A raster image is a grid made up of pixels,
and for each of these pixels, and for each

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:17.269
of the pixels, we specify a color.

00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:21.539
That's all there is in an image - it is nothing
but a collection of pixels.

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:26.809
All photographs on your phone, and generally
most images you encounter are raster images.

00:00:26.809 --> 00:00:31.739
It is easy to see that the quality of such
images greatly depends on the resolution of

00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:36.760
this grid - of course, the more grid points,
the finer the grid is, the more details we

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:37.760
can see.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.309
However, in return, if we disregard compression
techniques, the file size grows proportionally

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:48.250
to the number of pixels, and if we zoom in
too close, we shall witness these classic

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.040
staircase effects that we like to call aliasing.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.329
However, if we are designing a website, or
a logo for a company, which should look sharp

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:02.110
on all possible devices and zoom levels, vector
graphics is a useful alternative.

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:06.479
Vector images are inherently different from
raster images, as the base elements of the

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:10.830
image are not pixels, but vectors and control
points.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:16.040
The difference is like storing the shape of
a circle on a lot of pixels point by point,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.049
which would be a raster image, or just saying
that I want a circle on these coorindates

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:22.650
with a given radius.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:27.810
And as you can see in this example, the point
of this is to have razor sharp images at higher

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.469
zoom levels as well.

00:01:29.469 --> 00:01:35.159
Unless we go too crazy with fine details,
file sizes are also often remarkably small

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:40.090
for vector images, because we're not storing
the colors of millions of pixels.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.100
We are only storing shapes.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:47.689
If we want to sound a bit more journalistic
we can kind of say that vector images have

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:49.270
infinite resolution.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.979
We can zoom in as much as we wish, and we
won't lose any detail during this process.

00:01:54.979 --> 00:01:59.850
Vectorization is the process where we try
to convert a raster image to a vector image.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.520
Some also like to call this process image
tracing.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.240
The immediate question arises - why are we
not using vector graphics everywhere?

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.180
Well, one, the smoother the color transitions
and the more detail we have in our images,

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.850
the quicker the advantage of vectorization
evaporates.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.570
And two, also note that this procedure is
not trivial and we are also often at the mercy

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.370
of the vectorization algorithm in terms of
output quality.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.140
It is often unclear in advance whether it
will work well on a given input.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.000
So now we know everything we need to know
to be able to understand and appreciate this

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.800
amazing piece of work.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.870
The input is a rough sketch, that is a raster
image, and the output is a simplified, cleaned-up

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.630
and vectorized version of it.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.500
We're not only doing vectorization, but simplification
as well.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:55.480
This is a game changer, because this way,
we can lean on the additional knowledge that

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.120
these input raster images are sketches, hand-drawn
images, therefore there is a lot of extra

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.390
fluff in them that would be undesirable to
retain in the vectorized output, therefore

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:07.940
the name, sketch simplification.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:13.230
In each of these cases, it is absolute insanity
how well it works.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.360
Just look at these results!

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.280
The next question is obviously, how does this
wizardry happen?

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.180
It happens by using a classic deep learning
technique, a convolutional neural network,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.550
of course, that was trained on a large number
of input and output pairs.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:32.090
However, this is no ordinary convolutional
neural network!

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.500
This particular variant differs from the standard
well-known architecture as it is augmented

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.450
with a series of upsampling convolution steps.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:46.000
Intuitively, the algorithm learns a sparse
and concise representation of these input

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.290
sketches, this means that it focuses on the
most defining features and throws away all

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.780
the unneeded fluff.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:58.270
And the upsampling convolution steps make
it able to not only understand, but synthesize

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:03.470
new, simplified, and high-resolution images
that we can easily vectorize using standard

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.570
algorithms.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:08.400
It is fully automatic and requires no user
intervention.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.180
In case you are scratching your head about
these convolutions, we have had plenty of

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.799
discussions about this peculiar term before,
I have linked the appropriate episodes in

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:17.940
the video description box.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.969
I think you'll find them a lot of fun - in
one of them, I pulled out a guitar and added

00:04:22.969 --> 00:04:25.729
reverberation to it using convolution.

00:04:25.729 --> 00:04:31.159
It is clear that there is a ton of untapped
potential in using different convolution variations

00:04:31.159 --> 00:04:32.520
in deep neural networks.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:37.879
We've seen in a DeepMind paper earlier that
used dilated convolutions for state of the

00:04:37.879 --> 00:04:43.669
art speech synthesis, that is a novel convolution
variant and this piece of work is no exception

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:44.669
either.

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:49.000
There is also a cool online demo of this technique
that anyone can try.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.720
Make sure to post your results in the comments
section!

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.870
We'd love to have a look at your findings.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.960
Also, have a look at this Two Minute Papers
fan art.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.650
A nice little logo one of our kind Fellow
